# INSIDE a mirrored sphere. Nothing in the world but two objects and the light they
# make: the gallery's xenon short-arc lamp, and a vintage rangefinder camera -- and the
# rangefinder IS the camera we are looking out of.
#
# The render camera's entrance pupil is placed ON the mesh's own front lens element and
# aimed down the mesh's own optical axis, so the body, the bellows and the chrome barrel
# are all BEHIND the eye and never appear directly in frame. What you see of the camera
# is its REFLECTION -- which is the whole point of putting it inside a mirror.
#
# The render camera is INSIDE the sphere, so there is no outside, no room, no floor and
# no horizon -- the silver shell is the entire universe. A sphere seen from within is a
# CONCAVE mirror, so it does not just repeat the two objects, it wraps them: the lamp
# and the camera reappear inverted and smeared around the whole field of view, and every
# copy is lit by the same single arc.
#
# `look_at 0 0 0` aims the axis at the sphere's centre, so the central ray hits the shell
# perpendicular and RETROREFLECTS straight back down its own path: the camera photographs
# itself, dead centre. With f = R/2 = 0.20 m and an object distance u = |P| + R = 0.517 m,
# the self-portrait forms INVERTED at 0.63x, about 25 deg across, 0.19 m in front of the
# lens. The pupil sits near the sphere's centre on purpose -- magnification -> -1 as
# |P| -> 0, so a central eye makes the self-image as large as it can be (25 deg here
# versus ~9 deg if the camera hugged the wall).
#
#   ftrace -in scenes/mirror_sphere_interior.ftsl -mode D -max-bounce 64 -time 1800 \
#          -interval 30 -checkpoint -o png/mirrorsphere/mirrorsphere.png -window -keepwindow
#
# Then develop it about 3x brighter, straight off the checkpoint, no re-render:
#
#   ftrace -topng png/mirrorsphere/mirrorsphere.png.ftbuf \
#          png/mirrorsphere/mirrorsphere_ev3.png -ev 3
#
# The boost is not a fudge, it is forced by the scene: auto-exposure anchors on the
# 99th luminance percentile, and here that percentile IS the bare arc, which is orders
# of magnitude brighter than silver-bounced light. Anchoring to it leaves the shell at
# a median of 33/255. x3 puts the median at 60 and the recursive rings actually read,
# at the cost of clipping 1.7% of pixels -- essentially just the arc itself, which is
# a 6000 K point source and *should* blow out. (Until 0.102.1 `-topng` silently dropped
# -ev, so this took a full re-render; that fix is what makes the one-liner above work.)
#
# `-max-bounce 64` is NOT optional here, it is the single setting the whole image depends
# on. Mode D defaults to 8 edges; a 97%-reflective silver cavity needs ~33 before the light
# rattling around inside it has actually been absorbed, and the quartz envelope spends ~4
# vertices before a photon even gets out of the bulb. Measured on this exact frame, at
# equal render time:
#
#     -max-bounce  8   97% of pixels exactly 0.0   (a lamp floating in black)
#     -max-bounce 48   67%                          (the first ring of images appears)
#     -max-bounce 64   29%                          (the hall of mirrors)
#
# 64 is also the deepest the GPU kernel goes (BDPT_DEEPDEPTH in render_cuda.cu) -- the
# per-thread vertex stack is the binding constraint. Expect ~15x fewer samples/second than
# the default depth: the BDPT connection loop is O(depth^2). That is what the 1800 s is for.
# (Until 0.102.0 mode D hard-coded 8 and silently dropped -max-bounce, so this scene simply
# could not be rendered; the fix is what these numbers came out of.)
#
# A perfect mirror is a DELTA BSDF -- the reflected direction is not sampled, it is
# computed -- so the hall of mirrors itself costs almost no variance. The noise budget
# is spent entirely on the lamp: the arc sits behind a quartz wall, and you cannot
# next-event-connect a shading point to a light through a refracting interface, so every
# photon has to random-walk out through the glass. That is why this is mode D (BDPT) and
# not mode R -- the light subpath starts INSIDE the envelope and walks out, which is the
# only cheap way through.

scene { units meters  spectral 360 830 1 }

# --- the shell -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Measured Ag reflectance, mirror-smooth. Smooth and not satin on purpose: a rough lining
# blurs the recursive images into a flat grey soup after two bounces, which is exactly what
# an integrating sphere is designed to do and the opposite of the picture. The concentric
# rings of repeated lamps you see are the payoff, and they only survive a DELTA BSDF.
#
# Silver's reflectance is ~0.97, so the mean free path of a photon in here is ~1/(1-0.97)
# = 33 bounces. That number, not the geometry, is what sets the render settings: at the
# default depth of 8 this frame is 97% pure black, at 48 it is 67% black, at 64 it is 29%
# black and the hall of mirrors is actually there. See the -max-bounce note in the header.
material "mirrorskin" { type mirror  reflect metal:silver }
sphere { center 0 0 0  radius 0.40  material mirrorskin }

# --- the xenon short-arc lamp (verbatim from scenes/gallery.ftsl) ---------------
# Nested dielectrics, Schmidt-Budge priority: a quartz envelope with a higher-priority
# copy of the same mesh at 0.94x carving the interior to xenon gas at operating
# pressure, so the (1 - 0.94) gap IS the glass wall thickness.
material "lamp_quartz"   { type dielectric ior glass:fused-silica  priority 20 }
material "lamp_xenon"    { type dielectric ior glass:xenon-lamp    priority 30 }
material "lamp_electrode"{ type glossy reflect metal:chrome roughness 0.28 }
material "lamp_cordmat"  { type diffuse reflect rgb 0.05 0.05 0.06 }

# Native mesh orientation: electrode axis = X, cord = +Y. Gallery's 0.11 scale gives an
# envelope radius ~0.046 m -- a real short-arc envelope, and about a third of the
# camera's body width, which is the size relation that makes the pairing read.
#
# Placed 29.3 deg off the optical axis at 0.300 m, where its envelope subtends 8.7 deg of
# radius: the bulb's inner edge lands 20.6 deg out, clear of the 25-deg-wide self-image by
# 8 deg, so the lamp reads as a separate object sitting beside the camera's own portrait
# instead of blazing through it. The offset is swung mostly SIDEWAYS rather than up --
# the frame is 99 deg wide but only 76 deg tall, so the same angle that clipped off the
# top when aimed straight up lands the bulb comfortably inside the left edge.
#
# `rotate 0 25 0` swings the electrode axis (local X) from 50 deg off the view direction
# to 37 deg, foreshortening the long electrode stems to 60% of their broadside length so
# the whole lamp fits in frame instead of running out past the left edge. Not turned any
# further: end-on, the near electrode would eclipse the arc.
mesh "lamp_bulb" { file "assets/lamp/bulb.obj"  rotate 0 25 0  translate -0.017 0.132 -0.157  scale 0.11    material lamp_quartz }
mesh "lamp_xe"   { file "assets/lamp/bulb.obj"  rotate 0 25 0  translate -0.017 0.132 -0.157  scale 0.1034  material lamp_xenon }
mesh "lamp_elec" { file "assets/lamp/inner.obj" rotate 0 25 0  translate -0.017 0.132 -0.157  scale 0.11    material lamp_electrode }
# The cord is stretched only along its LENGTH (x/z stay at the assembly's 0.11) so the
# lamp actually hangs from the shell instead of trailing a pigtail into thin air:
# cord.obj is 1.0005 long, and 0.2354 lands its top at y=0.3675, which is exactly where
# the sphere's surface sits above (x,z) = (-0.017, -0.157). (The cord runs along local Y,
# which the Y-rotation leaves alone, so it still hangs straight up.)
mesh "lamp_cord" { file "assets/lamp/cord.obj"  rotate 0 25 0  translate -0.017 0.132 -0.157  scale 0.11 0.2354 0.11   material lamp_cordmat }

# The arc: a tiny emitter in the electrode gap at the mesh centre. This is the only
# light in the scene -- everything else you see is silver bouncing it back.
light sphere { center -0.017 0.132 -0.157   radius 0.008   spd blackbody 6000 }

# --- the vintage camera --------------------------------------------------------
# Purple folding rangefinder (FED/Zorki lineage), the same asset the `vintage` camera
# PRESET was specced from. The GLB carries its own PBR materials, so no material line.
# Raw mesh is 4.765 units across and the node transforms compose to unit scale, so
# 0.0285 puts the body at 0.136 m -- the real thing.
#
# THE PLACEMENT IS SOLVED, NOT EYEBALLED. The barrel is a surface of revolution about
# local (x,y) = (0.439, -0.190) running along local +Z, with the frontmost glass at
# z = 1.330. ftrace composes a mesh transform as scale, Rx, Ry, Rz, translate (see
# src/mesh.h), so the rotation below is the inverse solve that takes the mesh's local
# +Z onto the world viewing direction A = (0.641, 0.556, -0.530), and the translation
# is whatever then lands the front element exactly on the pupil P.
#   pupil P    = (-0.075, -0.065, 0.062),  |P| = 0.117
#   rotate     = -33.742 129.579 0
#   translate  = (-0.09373, -0.08163, 0.09372)
# Verified numerically: the front element lands on P to 5 decimals, the body's farthest
# point is |p| = 0.215 < R = 0.40 so nothing pokes through the shell, and the widest ray
# in a 76-deg cone is 49.5 deg off-axis -- under 90, so no part of the body clips the
# frame. (`preset vintage` was deliberately NOT used: its 27-deg coverage would fill the
# frame with the self-image and show nothing else.)
#
# The `material` line is a required FALLBACK, not the look: glTF/GLB import their own
# pbrMetallicRoughness materials by default (that is what carries the purple leatherette
# and the chrome barrel), and the FTSL material is only consulted under
# `import_materials no`.
material "camfallback" { type diffuse  reflect rgb 0.42 0.40 0.46 }
mesh "vcam" {
    file "cameras/vintage_camera.glb"
    material camfallback
    scale 0.0285
    rotate -33.742 129.579 0
    translate -0.09373 -0.08163 0.09372
}

# --- the taking camera: the eye IS the rangefinder's front element --------------
camera "cam" {
    eye -0.075 -0.065 0.062   look_at 0 0 0   up 0 1 0   fov_y 76
    mode D
    film { res 1100 733 }
}
